Round 0: Resume Shortlisting and Telephonic Screening

HR contacted me one evening over a call asking me if I applied for the frontend role. I was then asked to introduce myself following which I was informed that I had an interview scheduled the following day.

Round 1 : Technical(DSA + React) (35-40 minutes)

The interview began with the interviewer’s introduction. I was first asked to introduce myself and then to give more details about my projects. The first question was to write code to find the number of trailing zeroes in the factorial of a given number. I wrote the brute force code in C++ in an online compiler with separate functions. I was asked to analyze the complexity of the code and test it on several test cases. Then, I was asked to make changes such that it returns all the zeros instead of the trailing ones.

Then, I was provided with the formula for the above question. I was asked to code it and explain its complexity.

Next was the development part, where I was given an example of mobile phones where a certain window appears on clicking the Kernel version for n times. I was asked how to implement it with Reactjs (both the useEffect and useState hooks should be used). Then, there were more questions on the use of useState and useEffect hooks. Finally, there were some questions on javascript such as the difference between == and  ===.

The round ended after the questions from my side.

Round 2: React - Technical (30 minutes)

I was asked for an introduction and some details about my project following which the interviewer jumped straight into coding. I was given a picture of a certain layout with various blocks. My task was to create the layout on a webpage live on the meet. With a few challenges, I successfully did the task in 10 minutes.

Then, I was given a code snippet in React. I was asked what that code would do. The code was :

import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {

const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

useEffect(() => {

setCount(count+1);

},[]);

return (

You clicked {count} times

);

}

Initially, I answered it wrong saying the state would never change. Then, I was asked about the useEffect hook and when it is triggered. Upon realizing my mistake, I corrected my answer and explained it to him. I was then asked how to implement a certain feature on a button’s click. There was a discussion on my answer for about 10 minutes where I was asked to optimize my approach (basically avoid using useEffect when not necessary). The interview ended with a positive review from the interviewer’s side.

Round 3 : CEO round (15 minutes)

Out of the initial 10-12 students, only 3 made it to this round.

It was not much of a technical round, but only two out of the three would be selected.

The CEO asked me for my introduction and went on to ask general questions about my hometown. I could see he had my resume opened up in another tab, he asked about what Nextjs is and why do we use it? (I answered about server-side rendering). The next question was tricky where he asked what is the downside of server-side rendering or when not to use Nextjs. I didn’t know the answer to this and said whatever came off the top of my head. He then asked me to share my screen and google it in 3 minutes. I searched across a couple of websites but found no concrete answer. Then, I explained whatever I understood and luckily, he was satisfied with my response.

The next question was “What are you not good at ?”. I answered it thoroughly explaining my weakness (make sure to prepare for this question) along with relevant situations. I was also asked to tell about a scenario where I encountered a problem during my internship and how I tackled it.

The interview ended quickly and I wasn’t sure if I would be selected. Luckily, I received the mail two days later stating that I had been selected.

